### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants the second-largest value from a list. This tests distinct, sorted with reverse order, skip, findFirst, and whether you understand the semantics of 'second-largest'.

### How do you find the second-largest number?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;

public class SecondLargest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> amounts = List.of(
            50000, 125000, 75000, 125000, 90000, 50000);

        Optional<Integer> secondLargest = amounts.stream()
            .distinct()
            .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
            .skip(1)
            .findFirst();

        System.out.println("Second-largest: Rs."
            + secondLargest.map(String::valueOf).orElse("N/A"));
    }
}
```

```text
Second-largest: Rs.90000
```

### Why is distinct() critical?

```java
List<Integer> amounts = List.of(125000, 125000, 90000);

// WRONG: without distinct, second element is still 125000
Optional<Integer> wrong = amounts.stream()
    .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(1)
    .findFirst();
System.out.println("Without distinct: " + wrong.get());  // 125000!

// CORRECT: distinct collapses duplicate amounts
Optional<Integer> correct = amounts.stream()
    .distinct()
    .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(1)
    .findFirst();
System.out.println("With distinct: " + correct.get());  // 90000
```

### How do you handle edge cases?

```java
// Fewer than 2 distinct values: empty Optional
Optional<Integer> oneValue = List.of(100, 100, 100).stream()
    .distinct().sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(1).findFirst();
System.out.println(oneValue.isPresent());  // false

// Empty list: empty Optional
Optional<Integer> empty = List.<Integer>of().stream()
    .distinct().sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(1).findFirst();
System.out.println(empty.isPresent());  // false

// Two distinct values: returns the smaller one
Optional<Integer> two = List.of(100, 200).stream()
    .distinct().sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(1).findFirst();
System.out.println(two.get());  // 100
```

### How is this different from a single-pass O(n) approach?

| Approach | Time | Space | Clarity |
|---|---|---|---|
| distinct-sort-skip | O(n log n) | O(n) for sort buffer | High |
| Two-variable single pass | O(n) | O(1) | Medium |

```java
// O(n) single-pass approach
int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
int secondMax = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
for (int n : amounts) {
    if (n > max) {
        secondMax = max;
        max = n;
    } else if (n > secondMax && n != max) {
        secondMax = n;
    }
}
```

Use the stream approach for readability on moderate data; switch to the loop for performance-critical paths.

### What about using max twice?

```java
// Alternative: find max, then find max of the rest
Optional<Integer> maxVal = amounts.stream().max(Comparator.naturalOrder());

maxVal.flatMap(max -> amounts.stream()
    .filter(n -> n < max)
    .max(Comparator.naturalOrder()))
    .ifPresent(second ->
        System.out.println("Second: Rs." + second));
```

This also works and avoids sorting, but makes two passes.

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: using skip(1) without distinct.

```java
// WRONG: skip(1) skips the first element, not the first distinct value
// If max appears twice, second element is still max

// CORRECT: always use distinct() before sort-and-skip
```

Mistake: not handling the Optional when fewer than 2 values exist.

```java
// WRONG: get() on empty Optional throws NoSuchElementException
int wrong = amounts.stream().distinct()
    .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(1).findFirst().get();  // may throw!

// CORRECT: use orElse or ifPresent
int correct = amounts.stream().distinct()
    .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .skip(1).findFirst().orElse(-1);
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you find the Nth-largest?" Change skip(1) to skip(n-1).
- "How do you find the second-largest per department?" Group first, then apply the pattern within each group.
- "What if duplicates should count?" Remove distinct() so repeated values occupy separate positions.
